  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 242 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nThere is a potential for light rain or sprinkles Friday\nafternoon. There is a subtle area of warm air advection that\nwill slide across southern WI during the afternoon. While it \nwill be running into a region that has had persistent low level \ndry air in place, it is hard to ignore the consistent signal \nfrom the GFS/NAM/ECMWF models. The model blend introduced a \nslight chance of light rain to the forecast for this time \nperiod. The moisture source for the low levels is from a weak \nshortwave coming from the Pacific Northwest. It looks \nsufficiently deep enough to warrant the chance for light rain or\nsprinkles for a brief time period Friday afternoon and into the\nearly evening, with amounts of less than a tenth of an inch and\nno lightning.\n\nBreezy southerly winds will are expected Saturday. This will\npush temperatures well above normal, into the upper 70s to\naround 80, with continued warmth Sunday. A tropical disturbance\nover the Gulf of America is expected to strengthen and then\ntrack across the Ohio River Valley Sunday. Portions of \nsoutheast WI could get clipped by some light rain on the \noutskirts of this system, but cloud cover would be the more \nprobably threat (which would keep temps in the lower-mid 70s \ninstead of upper). \n\nOur next chance for rain will be along a cold frontal passage\nTuesday.\n\nCronce",
